<------------------------------------------------------Sorting--------------------------------------------------->
| Algorithm          | Best Case  | Average Case | Worst Case | Space Complexity | Stable? | Notes                         |
| ------------------ | ---------- | ------------ | ---------- | ---------------- | ------- | ----------------------------- |
| **Bubble Sort**    | O(n)       | O(n²)        | O(n²)      | O(1)             | Yes     | Compare adjacent elements     |
| **Selection Sort** | O(n²)      | O(n²)        | O(n²)      | O(1)             | No      | Finds min/max each pass       |
| **Insertion Sort** | O(n)       | O(n²)        | O(n²)      | O(1)             | Yes     | Good for nearly sorted arrays |
| **Merge Sort**     | O(n log n) | O(n log n)   | O(n log n) | O(n)             | Yes     | Divide & conquer              |
| **Quick Sort**     | O(n log n) | O(n log n)   | O(n²)      | O(log n)         | No      | Divide & conquer, pivot-based |
| **Heap Sort**      | O(n log n) | O(n log n)   | O(n log n) | O(1)             | No      | Uses max/min heap             |
| **Counting Sort**  | O(n+k)     | O(n+k)       | O(n+k)     | O(k)             | Yes     | Only for integers in a range  |
| **Radix Sort**     | O(nk)      | O(nk)        | O(nk)      | O(n+k)           | Yes     | Uses counting sort internally |
| **Shell Sort**     | O(n log n) | O(n^(3/2))   | O(n²)      | O(1)             | No      | Gap-based insertion sort      |


  <------------------------------------------------------Searching--------------------------------------------------->
  | Algorithm                | Best Case | Average Case | Worst Case | Space Complexity                    | Notes                                         |
| ------------------------ | --------- | ------------ | ---------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| **Linear Search**        | O(1)      | O(n)         | O(n)       | O(1)                                | Works on **unsorted arrays**                  |
| **Binary Search**        | O(1)      | O(log n)     | O(log n)   | O(1) iterative / O(log n) recursive | Requires **sorted array**                     |
| **Jump Search**          | O(1)      | O(√n)        | O(√n)      | O(1)                                | Works on sorted arrays                        |
| **Interpolation Search** | O(1)      | O(log log n) | O(n)       | O(1)                                | Works on sorted, uniformly distributed arrays |
| **Exponential Search**   | O(1)      | O(log n)     | O(log n)   | O(1)                                | Works on sorted arrays                        |
| **Fibonacci Search**     | O(1)      | O(log n)     | O(log n)   | O(1)                                | Works on sorted arrays                        |

  1️⃣ Best Case

Definition: The scenario where the algorithm does the minimum amount of work possible.

Sorting Example:

Insertion Sort: If the array is already sorted, it just checks each element and does no swaps → O(n).

Searching Example:

Linear Search: If the element you’re looking for is the first element, the search stops immediately → O(1).

✅ Think: “The algorithm is super lucky.”

2️⃣ Average Case

Definition: The typical scenario for the algorithm, assuming inputs are random or not specially arranged.

Sorting Example:

Quick Sort: On a random array, it usually splits the array fairly evenly → O(n log n).

Searching Example:

Linear Search: On average, you’ll check half the elements before finding the target → O(n/2) ≈ O(n).

✅ Think: “What usually happens if I pick a random array?”

3️⃣ Worst Case

Definition: The scenario where the algorithm does the maximum amount of work possible.

Sorting Example:

Quick Sort: If the array is already sorted and you always pick the first element as pivot → O(n²).

Searching Example:

Linear Search: If the element is at the end or not present, you check all n elements → O(n).

✅ Think: “The algorithm is really unlucky or the data is tricky.”

4️⃣ Space Complexity

Definition: The amount of extra memory the algorithm needs in addition to the input.

Sorting Example:

Merge Sort: Needs O(n) extra space to store temporary arrays.

Heap Sort: Works in-place → O(1) extra space.

Searching Example:

Binary Search (iterative): O(1) extra space.

Binary Search (recursive): O(log n) space because of recursion stack.

✅ Think: “How much extra room does the algorithm need?”

5️⃣ Stable? (Only for sorting)

Definition: A sorting algorithm is stable if it preserves the order of equal elements.

Example:

Array: [5a, 3, 5b, 2] → after sorting stably: [2, 3, 5a, 5b].

Stable Sorts: Bubble Sort, Merge Sort, Insertion Sort

Unstable Sorts: Quick Sort, Heap Sort, Selection Sort

✅ Think: “Do duplicates keep their original order?”
